    "q-78583": {
      "dom_id": "step-q78583",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Consider the matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$  Find $f(D)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}-3X+5.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-3X+5 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-3X+5I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(D)={D}^{2}-3D+5I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${D}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(D):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(D) & ={D}^{2}-3D+5I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-3+5 & -3+9+0 \\\\ -1+3+0 & 3+0+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
