    "q-130688": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130688",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Consider the matrix $K=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find $f(K)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}-4X+1.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-4X+1 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-4X+I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(K)={K}^{2}-4K+I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${K}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{2} & =K\\cdot K \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(K):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(K) & ={K}^{2}-4K+I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 7\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7-8+1 & 4-4-0 \\\\ 12-12+0 & 7-8+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
